<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#devanagari">Glossary</a> · Term · script</p><h1 class="enc-title">Devanagari</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Devanagari is an Indian script, written from left to right with a characteristic horizontal headline, in which each consonant carries an inherent vowel. It developed from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmi-script">Brahmi script</a> through the Nagari scripts of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the late first millennium CE. It is used for Hindi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a>, Nepali, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and many other languages and is one of the most widely used writing systems in the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/devanagari.jpg" alt="Devanagari" width="320" height="632" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Symphonicallyu · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Devanagari_Varnamala.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Devanagari belongs to the Brahmic family of scripts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. It descends from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmi-script">Brahmi script</a> of the third century BCE, which evolved into the Nagari script, which in turn gave rise to Devanagari and Nandinagari. The name combines deva with nagari, an adjective from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> nagara, 'town' or 'city', meaning 'urban'; the meaning of the prefix deva, 'heavenly' or 'god', remains unclear. The word Nagari for a script of northern India is attested by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-biruni">Al-Biruni</a> in the eleventh century, while the form Devanagari is attested by the eighteenth century at the latest.</p><p>Nagari was in regular use by the seventh century CE and was fully developed by about the end of the first millennium. Evidence of its spread includes the mid-eighth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pattadakal">Pattadakal</a> pillar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, which carries both Siddha Matrika script and an early Telugu-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> script, and the Kangra Jawalamukhi inscription in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himachal-pradesh">Himachal Pradesh</a>, written in both Sharada and Devanagari. The eleventh-century Udayagiri inscriptions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> and an inscribed brick of 1217 now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> attest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> in Nagari script.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Devanagari is a left-to-right abugida with 48 primary characters, 14 vowels and 34 consonants. It has no letter case. It favours symmetrical, rounded shapes within squared outlines and is recognised by the horizontal line, the shirorekha, along the top of full letters. Its orthography reflects pronunciation, and its letters follow a phonetic order based on the manner and place of articulation, called the varnamala, 'garland of letters'.</p><p>Successive consonants without a vowel between them may join as conjuncts. Many consonants that have a vertical stroke lose it in such clusters, so that t-va is written tva. The virama marks the absence of a vowel. A subscript dot, the nukta, adds sounds from Persian, Arabic and English to Hindi, and further signs serve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a>, Kashmiri and Sindhi. A single vertical bar, the danda, ends a sentence or half-verse and a double bar a full verse.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Devanagari has been used for over 120 languages, the most widespread being Hindi, and is one of the official scripts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>. Marathi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, Pali, Nepali, Konkani, Maithili and Santali are among the languages that use it as a primary or secondary script. Religiously educated people in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a> long used it to record and pass on information, alongside local scripts such as Modi, Kaithi and Mahajani that served administration and commerce.</p><p>Several methods romanise it. IAST is the academic standard for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and the de facto standard of printed publications, while the Hunterian system is the national system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The Unicode Standard defines four blocks for it, and InScript is the keyboard layout standardised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india">Government of India</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Devanagari” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Since the 19th century, with printing, <b>Devanagari</b> has become the usual script, and the order of its letters follows the classification of sounds by the ancient phoneticians.</blockquote><a href="/language/sanskrit">Sanskrit · Sounds, Script &amp; Grammar</a></li><li><blockquote>Thirty manuscripts at the Bhandarkar Oriental Research Institute, in the Sharada and <b>Devanagari</b> scripts, were collected in the 19th century; the oldest of them dates to 1464 CE, and they joined UNESCO's Memory of the World Register in 2007.</blockquote><a href="/work/rigveda">Rigveda · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>It was written in many scripts derived from Brahmi, among them Siddham, which carried Buddhist mantras to China and Japan, and since the 19th century chiefly in <b>Devanagari</b>.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-written-word">The Written Word</a></li><li><blockquote>Inscriptions and stone pillars, some in early Nepali written in <b>Devanagari</b>, record the kings and their donations.</blockquote><a href="/state/khasa-malla-kingdom">Khasa Malla kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
